# Seventeeth — Cursor Rules

## Project identity
This is the website for Seventeeth, a small independent game studio based in France.
Tagline: "We make games worth your time."
Values: craft, slowness, respect for the player's attention, independence.
Tone: quiet confidence. Never corporate, never hype. Think Simogo, Norm Architects, Offscreen Magazine.

## Stack
- Pure vanilla HTML, CSS, JavaScript. No frameworks, no bundlers, no dependencies.
- Single page: index.html + assets/ folder.
- Deployed via GitHub Pages. Keep it static and simple.
- Do NOT introduce npm, React, Vue, Tailwind, Bootstrap, or any external library unless explicitly asked.
- External fonts from Google Fonts are allowed.

## File structure
```
/
├── index.html         ← main and only page
├── legal.html
├── privacy.html
├── assets/
│   ├── *.svg          ← illustrations and logo
│   ├── *.png          ← game thumbnails
│   └── *.css          ← stylesheets (if extracted)
├── modex/             ← game subfolder
└── .cursorrules
```

## HTML conventions
- Semantic HTML5 elements only: <section>, <nav>, <main>, <footer>, <article>, <h1>-<h3>.
- No div soup. Every element must earn its place.
- Inline styles are forbidden. All styles go in CSS.
- Class names use kebab-case: .game-card, .hero-title, .nav-link.
- IDs only for anchor links (#games, #about, #contact) and JS hooks.
- Images always have meaningful alt attributes.
- Keep the HTML readable — no minification in source.

## CSS conventions
- CSS custom properties (variables) for all design tokens:
  --color-bg, --color-text, --color-accent, --color-muted
  --font-serif, --font-sans
  --space-xs, --space-sm, --space-md, --space-lg, --space-xl, --space-2xl
  --transition-default
- Mobile-first. Base styles for mobile, media queries for desktop.
- No !important. Ever.
- Animations: subtle and purposeful only. Respect prefers-reduced-motion.
- Use CSS Grid and Flexbox. No floats, no tables for layout.

## JavaScript conventions
- Vanilla JS only. No jQuery, no utility libraries.
- Use const by default, let when needed, never var.
- Keep JS minimal — if it can be done in CSS, do it in CSS.
- Event listeners use addEventListener, never inline onclick.
- No console.log left in production code.

## Design direction
The aesthetic target is: Simogo + Norm Architects + Offscreen Magazine.
- Lots of whitespace. Silence is intentional, not emptiness.
- Typography does the heavy lifting. One expressive serif for headings, one neutral sans for body.
- Max 2 accent colors used sparingly.
- No decorative elements that don't carry meaning.
- Transitions: 200-400ms ease, never bouncy or flashy.
- The site should feel like it was made by one person who cares deeply about every detail.

## Typography rules
- Heading font: a serif with character (ex: Playfair Display, Cormorant, or DM Serif Display).
- Body font: a clean sans-serif (ex: Inter, DM Sans, or system-ui as fallback).
- Font sizes use clamp() for fluid scaling — no fixed px breakpoints for type.
- Line-height: 1.6 for body, 1.1-1.2 for large headings.
- Letter-spacing: slight negative tracking on large headings (-0.02em to -0.04em).
- Never use more than 2 typefaces.

## Content rules
- Do NOT rewrite or alter any existing copy unless explicitly asked.
- The existing writing is intentional and well-crafted. Preserve it.
- Section names and anchor links (#games, #about, #contact) must remain unchanged.

## Performance
- No unused CSS or JS.
- Images should be referenced as-is (already in assets/). Do not move or rename files.
- Prefer SVG for illustrations and icons.
- Avoid loading fonts for weights that aren't used.

## What to always do
- Read the existing code before suggesting changes.
- Make surgical edits — change only what's needed.
- Explain what you changed and why in one short sentence after each edit.
- When in doubt, do less. Restraint is the point.

## What to never do
- Do not add a framework or build step.
- Do not add cookie banners, popups, or modals unless asked.
- Do not add animations that feel playful or bouncy — this is a quiet studio.
- Do not add stock icons from Font Awesome or similar — use SVG or CSS only.
- Do not increase the number of HTTP requests without a good reason.
- Do not change the file structure without asking first.
